    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required: on successful create, emit an audit line via the logger named `AUDIT` containing the owner id, the `customerCode` and the `registrationDate`.\n\nI made a single, minimal change to `SaveOwner.java` \u2014 the one step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline where all three values are populated (`customerCode` was assigned by the prior `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` step, `registrationDate` by `BuildOwner`, and `id` becomes available once the entity is persisted):\n\n```java\nprivate static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n\npublic void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {\n    ownerRepository.save(owner);\n    AUDIT.info(\"owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\", owner.getId(),\n        owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n}\n```\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:** `SaveOwner` is a single-method class, so `WMC_other` = 0 (\u21921), the method's cyclomatic complexity stays at 1 (no branches added), and only one file is touched. The audit lands on the success path since save must have succeeded for the line to run. `LocalDate.toString()` produces the same ISO `yyyy-MM-dd` form the JSON response uses, matching the test's `registrationDate` assertion.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` \u2014 and the whole suite \u2014 passes.",
